<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venantius Fortunatus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 530–c. 609</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Venantius Honorius Clementianus Fortunatus, Venance Fortunat, Saint Venantius Fortunatus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Venantius Fortunatus was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet and hymn writer at the Merovingian courts and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, venerated as a saint. Born between 530 and 540 near Treviso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and educated in Ravenna, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 566 and performed a poem at the wedding of King Sigibert and Queen Brunhild. At Poitiers he became a close friend of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a>, writing poems in her honour, and enjoyed the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>. His eleven surviving books of poetry include panegyrics and epitaphs, and his hymns Pange lingua gloriosi proelium certaminis and Vexilla Regis prodeunt entered the Catholic liturgy. He became Bishop of Poitiers around 599–600 and died early in the 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venantius-fortunatus.jpg" alt="Venantius Fortunatus" width="330" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venantius_Fortunatus_miniature.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Venantius Fortunatus was born at Duplavis, near Treviso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, and grew up during the Roman reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Historians disagree about where he spent his childhood. D. Tardi suggests that his family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> after the troubles that followed the death of King Theoderic, while Judith George argues that it never did, since the poet speaks of Duplavis more than of any other place. Sometime in the 550s or 60s he went to Ravenna, where he received a classical education in the Roman manner and became familiar with poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, Statius and Martial, as well as Christian writers including Arator, Claudian and Coelius Sedulius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Fortunatus moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in the spring of 566, probably intending to become a poet at the Merovingian court. He gave two reasons for his winding route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>: the adventures of a wandering minstrel, and a wish to venerate Saint Martin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. At Metz he performed a poem for the whole court at the marriage of King Sigibert and Queen Brunhild, and nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> then sought verse from him. He visited King Charibert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> until Charibert's death in 567 or 568, and later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a>.</p><p>He became her close friend and wrote many poems in her honour and in support of her political campaigns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, installed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 573, became a patron, and in 580 Fortunatus wrote a poem defending him against charges of treason at the court of King Chilperic. He was ordained around 576 and stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, succeeding Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> about 599 to 600.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Fortunatus left eleven books of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poetry, including epitaphs, panegyrics, georgics, consolations and religious verse, arranged chronologically and by the importance of the subject. He wrote four major panegyrics for Merovingian kings. The wedding poem for Sigibert and Brunhild tells how Cupid brought the couple together, in the manner of the classical poets. Scholars differ on the poem for Chilperic, which praises a headstrong king as gracious and merciful: some see an attempt to please a new patron, others a gentle hint of how an ideal king should rule, made to help Gregory.</p><p>His long narrative poem Vita S. Martini and a verse life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a> belong to his other work. His hymns Pange lingua and Vexilla Regis prodeunt entered the liturgy; the latter honours a relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> sent to Radegund by the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a>. He wrote as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> verse moved from quantitative metre towards accentual forms, and his poems give a view of the Merovingian court that sometimes differs from Gregory's account.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Venantius Fortunatus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its author is unknown: it has been attributed to <b>Venantius Fortunatus</b> in the 6th century, Hermannus Contractus in the 11th and Bernard of Clairvaux in the 12th.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ave-maris-stella">Ave maris stella</a></li><li><blockquote>Its opening words echo an older and famous Latin hymn, &quot;Pange lingua gloriosi proelium certaminis&quot; by <b>Venantius Fortunatus</b>, from which it is derived.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Valdobbiadene, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.92,12.01&amp;year=581" aria-label="Valdobbiadene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Venantius Fortunatus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5710 6128h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5710 6128h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.92,12.01&amp;year=581">What was here in 581 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
